## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a practical, event-driven data pipeline with CSV parsing, per-record processing, PII masking, and cost-conscious AWS resource usage.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event triggers and Lambda functions
- CSV parsing and per-message processing
- Amazon Comprehend: sentiment analysis and entity detection
- PII masking rules and data privacy compliance
- S3 encryption, IAM least privilege, and dead-letter queues (DLQ)
- DynamoDB indexing for conversation metadata

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPII(text) {
  const emailRegex = /\b[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Z|a-z]{2,}\b/g;
  const phoneRegex = /\b\d{3}[-.]?\d{3}[-.]?\d{4}\b/g;
  return text
    .replace(emailRegex, '[EMAIL_REDACTED]')
    .replace(phoneRegex, '[PHONE_REDACTED]');
}

async function processTranscript(record) {
  const bucket = record.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = decodeURIComponent(record.s3.object.key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
  const response = await s3.getObject({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key }).promise();
  const csvContent = response.Body.toString();
  const rows = parseCSV(csvContent);
  const messages = rows.map(row => ({
    role: row.role,
    timestamp: row.timestamp,
    message: maskPII(row.message)
  }));
  return messages;
}

async function analyzeSentiment(text) {
  const result = await comprehend.detectSentiment({
    Text: text,
    LanguageCode: 'en'
  }).promise();
  return result.Sentiment;
}

async function detectEntities(text) {
  const result = await comprehend.detectEntities({
    Text: text,
    LanguageCode: 'en'
  }).promise();
  return result.Entities;
}
```

